        {"h2_html": "The honest case for <em>separate point tools</em>",
         "body_html": '<p style="color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.85;max-width:64ch">Best-of-breed tools earn their reputation. A dedicated scheduler, a dedicated review platform, a dedicated phone service, each can go deeper in its one job than any all-in-one, and you get to pick your favorite for every slot and swap one out without touching the rest. If you have the time or the staff to run software and you want the deepest possible tool in each category, a stack of point tools is a legitimate choice.</p><p style="color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.85;max-width:64ch;margin-top:1rem">The cost shows up in the seams. Each tool is its own subscription, its own login, and its own learning curve, and they do not naturally talk to each other. The missed call your phone app logs does not automatically start the follow-up in your CRM, and the finished job in one tool does not trigger the review request in another. Someone has to be the glue between them, and for a busy owner that someone is usually you, at the end of a long day, which is exactly when the stitching does not happen and a lead goes cold.</p>'},
